⟦P0⟧Go ⟦P1⟧ Policy⟦P2⟧⟦P3⟧Back to Go Vulnerability Management⟦PB1_4⟧⟦P5⟧Overview⟦P6⟧The Go ⟦P7⟧ is a⟦P8⟧⟦P9⟧⟦P10⟧ Numbering Authority⟦PB1_11⟧, which issues⟦P12⟧⟦P13⟧⟦P14⟧ IDs⟦PB1_15⟧ and publishes⟦P16⟧⟦P17⟧⟦P18⟧ Records⟦PB1_19⟧⟦P20⟧for public vulnerabilities in the Go ecosystem. It is a sub-CNA of the Google ⟦P21⟧.⟦P22⟧Scope⟦P23⟧The Go ⟦P24⟧ covers vulnerabilities in the Go project (the Go⟦P25⟧⟦P26⟧standard library⟦PB1_27⟧ and⟦P28⟧⟦P29⟧sub-repositories⟦PB1_30⟧) and public vulnerabilities⟦P31⟧in importable Go modules that are not already covered by another ⟦P32⟧.⟦P33⟧This scope is intended to explicitly exclude vulnerabilities in applications or⟦P34⟧packages written in Go that are not importable (for example, anything in⟦P35⟧package ⟦P36⟧). See ⟦P37⟧⟦P38⟧⟦PB1_39⟧ for more information on excluded reports.⟦P40⟧To report potential new vulnerabilities in the Go project, refer to⟦P41⟧⟦P42⟧⟦P43⟧⟦PB1_44⟧.⟦P45⟧Requesting a ⟦P46⟧ ⟦P47⟧ for a public vulnerability⟦P48⟧⟦P49⟧IMPORTANT⟦P50⟧: The form linked below creates a public issue on the issue tracker, and therefore⟦P51⟧⟦P52⟧must not⟦P53⟧ be used to report undisclosed vulnerabilities in Go (see our⟦P54⟧⟦P55⟧security policy⟦PB1_56⟧ for instructions on reporting⟦P57⟧undisclosed issues).⟦P58⟧To request a ⟦P59⟧ ⟦P60⟧ for an existing PUBLIC vulnerability in the Go ecosystem,⟦P61⟧⟦P62⟧submit a request via this form⟦PB1_63⟧.⟦P64⟧A vulnerability is considered public if it has already been disclosed publicly, or it exists in a⟦P65⟧package you maintain, and you are ready to disclose it publicly.⟦P66⟧
